@anon.9bf146a73cb71d9c7e3fe69f46c42f4a.492 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [3857 x i8] }> <{ [3857 x i8] c"Finds elements in the document.\0A\0AThe `query` functions lets you search your document for elements of a\0Aparticular type or with a particular label. To use it, you first need to\0Aensure that [context] is available.\0A\0A# Finding elements\0AIn the example below, we create a custom page header that displays the text\0A\22Typst Academy\22 in small capitals and the current section title. On the\0Afirst page, the section title is omitted because the header is before the\0Afirst section heading.\0A\0ATo realize this layout, we open a `context` and then query for all headings\0Aafter the [current location]($here). The code within the context block\0Aruns twice: Once per page.\0A\0A- On the first page the query for all headings before the current location\0A  yields an empty array: There are no previous headings. We check for this\0A  case and just display \22Typst Academy\22.\0A\0A- For the second page, we retrieve the last element from the query's result.\0A  This is the latest heading before the current position and as such, it is\0A  the heading of the section we are currently in. We access its content\0A  through the `body` field and display it alongside \22Typst Academy\22.\0A\0A```example\0A>>> #set page(\0A>>>   width: 240pt,\0A>>>   height: 180pt,\0A>>>   margin: (top: 35pt, rest: 15pt),\0A>>>   header-ascent: 12pt,\0A>>> )\0A#set page(header: context {\0A  let elems = query(\0A    selector(heading).before(here()),\0A  )\0A  let academy = smallcaps[\0A    Typst Academy\0A  ]\0A  if elems.len() == 0 {\0A    align(right, academy)\0A  } else {\0A    let body = elems.last().body\0A    academy + h(1fr) + emph(body)\0A  }\0A})\0A\0A= Introduction\0A#lorem(23)\0A\0A= Background\0A#lorem(30)\0A\0A= Analysis\0A#lorem(15)\0A```\0A\0AYou can get the location of the elements returned by `query` with\0A[`location`]($content.location).\0A\0A# A word of caution { #caution }\0ATo resolve all your queries, Typst evaluates and layouts parts of the\0Adocument multiple times. However, there is no guarantee that your queries\0Acan actually be completely resolved. If you aren't careful a query can\0Aaffect itself\E2\80\94leading to a result that never stabilizes.\0A\0AIn the example below, we query for all headings in the document. We then\0Agenerate as many headings. In the beginning, there's just one heading,\0Atitled `Real`. Thus, `count` is `1` and one `Fake` heading is generated.\0ATypst sees that the query's result has changed and processes it again. This\0Atime, `count` is `2` and two `Fake` headings are generated. This goes on and\0Aon. As we can see, the output has a finite amount of headings. This is\0Abecause Typst simply gives up after a few attempts.\0A\0AIn general, you should try not to write queries that affect themselves. The\0Asame words of caution also apply to other introspection features like\0A[counters]($counter) and [state].\0A\0A```example\0A= Real\0A#context {\0A  let elems = query(heading)\0A  let count = elems.len()\0A  count * [= Fake]\0A}\0A```\0A\0A# Command line queries\0AYou can also perform queries from the command line with the `typst query`\0Acommand. This command executes an arbitrary query on the document and\0Areturns the resulting elements in serialized form. Consider the following\0A`example.typ` file which contains some invisible [metadata]:\0A\0A```typ\0A#metadata(\22This is a note\22) <note>\0A```\0A\0AYou can execute a query on it as follows using Typst's CLI:\0A```sh\0A$ typst query example.typ \22<note>\22\0A[\0A  {\0A    \22func\22: \22metadata\22,\0A    \22value\22: \22This is a note\22,\0A    \22label\22: \22<note>\22\0A  }\0A]\0A```\0A\0AFrequently, you're interested in only one specific field of the resulting\0Aelements. In the case of the `metadata` element, the `value` field is the\0Ainteresting one. You can extract just this field with the `--field`\0Aargument.\0A\0A```sh\0A$ typst query example.typ \22<note>\22 --field value\0A[\22This is a note\22]\0A```\0A\0AIf you are interested in just a single element, you can use the `--one`\0Aflag to extract just it.\0A\0A```sh\0A$ typst query example.typ \22<note>\22 --field value --one\0A\22This is a note\22\0A```" }>, align 1
